andard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-27T15:05:50.802Z", "request_id": "req_011CgSworAEPAK8e4vohj21x", "thinking_duration_ms": 140731}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgSworQrs8zxi15xewjg5", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Choice: candidate 3, probability it is the best ≈ 0.65.** Everything below is mental arithmetic on simplified models, as the rules require, so the figures are approximate.\n\n**1. Shortages at the start are the same for every plan.** The first order of item 1 arrives only on day 5. The starting 60 units cannot cover days 0–4, where forecast demand is about 189. Item 0 is short by a few units on days 0–1. These losses don't affect the comparison.\n\n**2. The order multiplier matters most.** The plans don't react to actual demand, and leftovers carry over to later days. So stock behaves like a random walk whose drift is the multiplier minus 1.046 (the average demand multiplier).\n- **Multiplier ×1.15:** about 10% extra arrives every day. Over 25–28 days, stock of item 1 builds to about 100+ units and item 0 to about 75–80. Holding costs about 1 per day for item 1 and 0.6 for item 0, and the excess is paid for anyway.\n- **Multiplier ×1.05:** drift is near zero. A few more units are lost to shortages (about 44 of item 1 and 30 of item 0 over the horizon), but holding costs are several times lower.\n\nEstimated cost (purchases + 13 or 18 per lost unit of item 1 or 0 + holding) compared with ×1.05:\n\n| | ×1.05 | ×1.10 | ×1.15 | ×1.20 |\n|---|---|---|---|---